{
  "id": "imperatives-lets",
  "level": "a1",
  "area": "Here and now",
  "title": "Imperatives and Let's",
  "tagline": "Give instructions, make rules and suggest things to do together.",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "exam": [
    "Reading Part 1 — signs and notices",
    "Writing Part 6 — a short email with instructions or an invitation"
  ],
  "why": "Imperatives are everywhere — on signs, in recipes, in classroom instructions and in games. They're also the shortest sentences in English: no subject, just the verb. <i>Let's</i> is how you suggest doing something together, perfect for planning a party or a game with friends.",
  "hook": {
    "text": "It's Mateo's birthday! \"<b>Let's</b> make a cake,\" says Sofia. \"<b>Open</b> the flour first. <b>Don't forget</b> the sugar!\" \"<b>Let's not</b> put too much chocolate,\" laughs Liam, \"or Miss Vega won't eat it!\"",
    "ask": "Find the instructions in the story. Which ones tell you to do something, and which one tells you NOT to do something?"
  },
